{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_07c56478fb5b87d50169bb40df278081918ea2a190c802ec1b", "created": 1773879519, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Integrative Oncology and Complementary/Alternative Therapies in Cancer Care\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on the use of complementary and alternative therapies (CAT) in the context of cancer treatment and the broader framework of integrative oncology. Complementary and alternative therapies are described as nonstandard supportive practices and products intended to be used alongside conventional cancer care to support comfort, survivorship needs, and quality of life, including side-effect management. Integrative oncology is explicitly defined as combining conventional (standard) cancer treatment with complementary approaches. The network links CAT to a set of specific modalities (e.g., acupuncture, dietary supplements, yoga, massage therapy, hypnosis, reiki, homeopathy, herbal preparations, eastern medicine, and medical marijuana) and also indicates that hospitals may provide meditation services to cancer patients. Overall, the community depicts a supportive-care ecosystem adjacent to standard oncology, with emphasis on adjunctive use rather than replacement of conventional treatment. [Data: Entities (6315, 6352, 6360, 4364, 6342); Relationships (14522, 14535, 14536, 9162, 14523, +more)]\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Complementary and alternative therapies are positioned as adjuncts to cancer treatment for comfort and side-effect support\",\"explanation\":\"Complementary and alternative therapies are characterized as “additional, nonstandard supportive practices and products” that may be used alongside conventional cancer care, with potential benefits for survivorship issues, comfort, and overall well-being during and after treatment. They are described as potentially helping individuals manage side effects and improve quality of life, explicitly framed as supportive options rather than replacements for standard medical therapies. [Data: Entities (6315); Relationships (14522)]\\n\\nThe explicit linkage of CAT to “cancer treatment” reinforces the community’s orientation toward symptom management and supportive care in oncology, rather than disease-directed replacement therapy. [Data: Relationships (14522)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Integrative oncology functions as the bridge that formally combines conventional treatment with complementary approaches\",\"explanation\":\"Integrative oncology is defined as an approach to cancer care that combines conventional (standard) cancer treatment with complementary and alternative therapies. This positions integrative oncology as the organizing concept that connects the conventional oncology domain with supportive complementary modalities. [Data: Entities (6352); Relationships (14535, 14536)]\\n\\nThe relationship data further emphasizes that integrative oncology “explicitly combines” standard cancer treatment with complementary approaches, anchoring the community’s structure around combination and coordination rather than substitution. [Data: Relationships (14535, 14536)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Conventional (standard) cancer treatment is treated as the foundational modality in the community\",\"explanation\":\"Conventional (standard) cancer treatment is described as mainstream evidence-based care (e.g., surgery, chemotherapy and other standard modalities) and is referenced as the foundation of treatment that may be combined with complementary therapies in integrative oncology. [Data: Entities (6360); Relationships (14536)]\\n\\nThis framing indicates that the community’s complementary practices are situated within a conventional-care baseline, with integrative oncology acting as a model for combining supportive approaches with established treatment pathways. [Data: Entities (6352, 6360); Relationships (14536)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community includes a diverse set of complementary modalities spanning mind-body, physical, and ingestible product categories\",\"explanation\":\"The CAT node is connected to multiple examples of complementary practices/products, including acupuncture, dietary supplements, eastern medicine, medical marijuana, herbal teas and preparations, homeopathy, hypnosis, reiki, yoga, and massage therapy. This demonstrates a broad taxonomy of modalities rather than reliance on a single practice. [Data: Entities (6315, 6342, 6343, 6344, 6345); Relationships (14523, 14524, 14525, 14526, 14527, +more)]\\n\\nSeveral of these modalities are explicitly described in supportive terms: acupuncture for symptom/side-effect relief; yoga for stress reduction and general well-being; massage therapy for relaxation and discomfort relief; hypnosis for anxiety/distress and symptom perception; and reiki for relaxation and comfort. [Data: Entities (6342, 6350, 6351, 6348, 6349)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Dietary supplements and herbal preparations are identified as commonly considered complementary products with interaction considerations\",\"explanation\":\"Dietary supplements are described as ingestible products (vitamins, minerals, etc.) used as complementary approaches that may affect symptoms or interact with treatment. Herbal teas and preparations are described as plant-based products used for perceived health or symptom benefits and are noted as requiring caution for interactions. [Data: Entities (6343, 6346)]\\n\\nThe inclusion of interaction cautions indicates that, within this community, at least some complementary products are recognized as potentially relevant to clinical management due to possible effects on symptoms and treatment compatibility. [Data: Entities (6343, 6346); Relationships (14524, 14527)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Medical marijuana is included as a complementary option for symptom relief under medical guidance\",\"explanation\":\"Medical marijuana is described as